<div class="welcome-letter-body">
    <h2 class="welcome-letter-kicker">You + {{ $appName }}</h2>
    <p class="welcome-letter-tagline">Together, you may create positive changes that reach beyond you and your child.</p>

    <p>As a parent, when your family is affected by divorce or separation, you are holding a lot for yourself and your child. You may be managing difficult family transitions and the everyday work of parenting all at once. Divorce or separation may be part of your story, but it is not your entire story. Although I do not know you personally, I know that you are a whole person with strength, dignity, wisdom, and the capacity to begin again tomorrow, if today feels hard. I know that because I have practiced and taught mindfulness for many years, and I am always reminded of the inner resources that we all have, but we easily forget.</p>

    <p>{{ $appName }} is the first self-guided mindfulness app systematically designed for parents who are affected by divorce or separation. It was created to introduce mindfulness skills that remind you about that whole person that you are. Don't over-think about the word “mindfulness”. It is not about forcing yourself to feel a certain way or denying how you are already feeling. It is simply a gentle invitation to rediscover your inner resources, including your capacity to pause, become aware, care for yourself, and bring intention to your next actions, including your interactions with your child and with your ex-partner.</p>

    <p>By opening this app, you are making space for your own well-being. That matters. The care you offer yourself can become part of the care you bring to your children. Mindfulness is not simply a positive affirmation. Rather, it is something you come to make it your own through embodied practices and experience. That is why {{ $appName }} includes opportunities to practice, explore, and discover what may be helpful for you.</p>

    <p><strong>If you ever wonder, “Is this for me?”</strong></p>
    <p>That is natural, especially when an activity feels unfamiliar, challenging, or brings you into contact with difficult feelings. You might pause and ask yourself, “What kinds of support do I need to continue, and how can I get that support?” If it feels right for you, we encourage you to explore the full program before deciding what it may offer.</p>

    <p>{{ $appName }} has been tested with parents navigating separation and divorce. Some have shared their experiences in testimonials on our website. Their journeys are not identical to yours, but they may remind you that you are not alone.</p>

    <p>No app can address every circumstance, but we worked on making this app accessible and helpful for parents across different situations. Every family has its own story. Some are navigating relatively cooperative relationships. Others are facing high conflict, toxic relationship dynamics, repeated court orders and motions, blended-family challenges, financial strain, a child with a disability or other special needs, or even taking care of an aging parent at the same time. Some parents have a lot of help, while others have few resources and little support.</p>

    <h3>Reminders</h3>

    <p><strong>No perfect way to practice, but your own way:</strong> When using this app, remember that you may follow the practice as it is offered, adapt it to your needs, pause, or return another day. The goal is not to complete every activity perfectly, clear your mind, or feel calm all the time. The goal is to discover what supports you and gradually bring it into your life in ways that feel meaningful and sustainable.</p>

    <p><strong>{{ $appName }} is a new source of support:</strong> {{ $appName }} is not intended to replace other kinds of support that's available to you. If you need additional help including legal, financial, emotional, or medical support, that does not mean you have failed or that you cannot benefit from the {{ $appName }} app. Please take care of yourself. Reaching out to a therapist, physician, support group, trusted friend, family member, attorney, or another professional can be important. If you ever feel unsafe or unable to cope, please seek immediate support through local crisis or emergency services.</p>

    <p><strong>When difficult feelings arise:</strong> During meditation or self-reflection, some people notice strong emotions, memories, body sensations, or discomfort. If this happens to you, it does not necessarily mean you are doing anything wrong. When we pause rather than push difficult experiences away, we can become more aware of what we have been carrying. If difficult feelings arrive, please practice with care and respect for your own limits.</p>

    <p><strong>Self-care is important:</strong> One thing to note here is that when using the app to do the activities, remember to make yourself comfortable: wear loose clothing that allows easy breathing and movement, dress in layers if needed, and consider eating lightly and staying hydrated before practice. Most importantly, listen to your body. You may change positions, stand up, stretch, lie down, open your eyes, shorten a practice, or take a break at any time. If emotions or sensations become overwhelming, you may stop and return only if and when you feel ready again. Caring for yourself is not a failure to practice; it is part of the practice.</p>

    <h3>Thank you for being here</h3>

    <p>We hope the app supports you. Remember, you do not need to have everything figured out before you begin. You only need to begin where you are, with this breath, this moment. The care you offer yourself today can become part of the care you bring to your children tomorrow.</p>

    <p>We are honored to walk alongside you.</p>
